WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.040 --> 00:00:08.080
 

00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:09.000
Ryan: Hey big man, how ya doing?

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:10.000
What's going on?

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:12.160
Michael: Not much.

00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:15.309
Ryan: Tell me about this backdrop,
it's very bricky.

00:00:15.849 --> 00:00:21.000
Michael: Yeah, you know, we have earthquakes in LA
so we like to put bricks in our buildings.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:22.000
Doesn't that make sense?

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:23.770
Ryan: Ton of sense.

00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:29.000
Michael: Cool man, well thanks for doing this.
I really appreciate it.

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:31.000
Ryan: My pleasure.

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:32.000
Michael: You ready to go?

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:34.000
Ryan: Yeah let's do it.

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:37.800
Michael: Cool, so I'm Michael Schneider

00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:42.100
I'm the founder and CEO of Service,
and this is the first edition of "Please Hold"

00:00:42.670 --> 00:00:47.740
which is a new podcast we're doing to
talk about business, life, and the sexy world

00:00:47.740 --> 00:00:51.820
of customer service.
Our first guest today is Ryan Holmes

00:00:51.820 --> 00:00:55.390
the CEO and founder of Hootsuite.
Ryan, thanks for being here.

00:00:55.900 --> 00:00:58.960
Ryan: Thanks for having me.
I can't believe it, I'm the first guest?

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:00.000
Michael: You're the first guest, man.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:02.000
Ryan: This is so exciting, I had no idea.

00:01:02.620 --> 00:01:05.409
Michael: Yeah, we're going to be able to look back
a year from now after hundreds of

00:01:05.409 --> 00:01:07.990
episodes and you're going to be number one.

00:01:08.060 --> 00:01:11.000
Ryan: It's incredible, thank you so much.
It's an honor.

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:11.620
Michael: Thank you.

00:01:11.620 --> 00:01:14.979
So I have a bunch of questions for you

00:01:14.979 --> 00:01:19.600
and the first thing I wanted to ask you is
really about the way you grew up because

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:24.670
my understanding is you grew up on a farm
with some electricity or no electricity

00:01:24.670 --> 00:01:26.500
but certainly not a lot of technology

00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:31.060
and I'm curious how someone
that grows up on a farm kind of in the

00:01:31.060 --> 00:01:35.710
middle of nowhere with barely having
electricity comes to start and run one

00:01:35.710 --> 00:01:38.290
of the most important technology
companies in social media.

00:01:38.290 --> 00:01:46.000
Ryan: Well, yeah I guess it is a bit of a paradigm isn't it,
or a bit of a contrast.

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:52.450
I grew up in the Okanagan in Canada,
so Western Canada, and yeah my

00:01:52.450 --> 00:01:56.200
parents decided to get a hobby farm

00:01:56.200 --> 00:01:59.830
so it was a very remote childhood.

00:01:59.830 --> 00:02:01.230
Michael: What's a hobby farm?

00:02:01.230 --> 00:02:05.850
Ryan: A hobby farm is, it wouldn't be a farm for commerce it

00:02:05.850 --> 00:02:12.420
would be a farm for yourself.
It's your hobby you're not selling

00:02:12.420 --> 00:02:15.500
a lot of product off of it which
would be a commercial venture.

00:02:15.500 --> 00:02:21.390
So yeah, we grew up on this farm and there were
goats and chickens and honeybees

00:02:21.390 --> 00:02:23.580
and a well

00:02:23.580 --> 00:02:29.400
kerosene lamps and it was very "Little
House in the Prairie" and was an amazing

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:35.400
place to grow up and to your point,
I discovered technology

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:41.000
and all these amazing things
like electrical lights and ultimately computers.

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:45.570
I got so excited about that, and you know, it's funny

00:02:45.570 --> 00:02:49.260
I guess sometimes you do the
opposite of what your parents do and

00:02:49.260 --> 00:02:54.330
I've thoroughly embraced technology and
long story short

00:02:54.330 --> 00:02:57.300
I'm now deeply in it.

00:02:57.300 --> 00:03:00.000
Michael: What was your first experience?
Was it some video game console?

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:03.000
Were you running a bulletin board?
Were you programming in Basic?

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:05.000
What hooked you into technology?

00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:12.000
Ryan: Well, actually it started in grade 5,
I went to school library

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:15.810
and they had all these fun things there.
Computers where, you know

00:03:15.810 --> 00:03:22.800
there's Apple IIc and 2 Plus computers,
and I was immediately hooked

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:27.420
like a moth to a flame.
I started playing with these computers

00:03:27.420 --> 00:03:32.000
starting with games, educational games that they had

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:36.000
but realized pretty quickly,
and the librarian was a great guy

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:42.900
super influential in my path and
he let me know that you can actually make games.

00:03:42.900 --> 00:03:44.910
You can learn how to program and build them

00:03:44.910 --> 00:03:49.860
and he taught me a little bit about Basic
and I just got even more hooked.

00:03:49.860 --> 00:03:51.690
I started playing with them all the time.

00:03:51.690 --> 00:03:56.880
As I start programming more

00:03:56.880 --> 00:04:01.260
he one day let me know that there was a
school district wide computer

00:04:01.260 --> 00:04:07.300
programming contest that was going on
and the grand prize was an Apple IIc computer

00:04:07.320 --> 00:04:10.190
It's probably about a $3,000 computer back in

00:04:10.190 --> 00:04:16.100
we're talking about 1985 here or 1986,
and so it was a monster prize.

00:04:16.880 --> 00:04:21.320
So I entered into it. He asked if I wanted to
go into this contest and I said "absolutely."

00:04:22.640 --> 00:04:31.200
So I did go into it, entered. I ended up
winning the the computer which was mind-blowing.

00:04:31.280 --> 00:04:36.770
I took it home but I had a problem which
you probably can see already and that

00:04:36.770 --> 00:04:42.230
was that we didn't have any electricity.
So my dad being the great guy that he is

00:04:42.230 --> 00:04:45.440
he took the computer and
went to an electronics store.

00:04:45.440 --> 00:04:49.970
He got it rewired and so every day I'd
come home after school and I'd pop the

00:04:49.970 --> 00:04:54.260
trunk on my mom's car and I take a
couple of alligator clips and plug it

00:04:54.260 --> 00:04:59.000
onto the car battery and there'd be a
line going into the house and I'd play

00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:01.000
on the computer until the wee hours and program away.

00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:10.640
So, you know, that was my early story about
my passion for computers and I did

00:05:10.640 --> 00:05:13.790
a number of different businesses through
the years that ultimately led me back

00:05:13.790 --> 00:05:20.090
into software and where my
12 year old self was passionate.

00:05:20.090 --> 00:05:23.570
Michael: That's awesome man.
You really had to work for it to be on a computer.

00:05:23.570 --> 00:05:24.860
That's really cool.

00:05:24.860 --> 00:05:29.200
So fast forward to today,
what is your daily tech routine?

00:05:29.200 --> 00:05:33.800
When you first wake up in the morning,
are you on email? Are you on social media?

00:05:33.800 --> 00:05:35.600
Is your phone off?

00:05:35.600 --> 00:05:38.780
What do you do to kind of
ramp up into the day?

00:05:38.780 --> 00:05:43.800
Ryan: Yeah, you know, I love mobile.

00:05:43.800 --> 00:05:51.000
I love how it gives us freedom but
it gives us accountability as well.

00:05:51.000 --> 00:05:52.910
I think there's an interesting
balance there.

00:05:52.910 --> 00:05:55.940
I do wake up. I do roll over.

00:05:55.940 --> 00:06:02.060
I check my Inbox. I check social media.
I'm primarily just doing a little

00:06:02.060 --> 00:06:09.700
check to see if there's any fires going on.
Did anything blow up in my sleep?

00:06:09.710 --> 00:06:13.640
Is there anything that I need to know?
Highly urgent stuff.

00:06:13.640 --> 00:06:19.200
I think the great thing, the liberating thing, is I can just do a quick check

00:06:19.220 --> 00:06:20.600
nothing going on there

00:06:20.600 --> 00:06:26.600
and then I can kind of get on with my day.
I usually do a bit of reading in bed

00:06:26.600 --> 00:06:31.700
seeing what's going on the world,
getting caught up, finding interesting articles

00:06:31.700 --> 00:06:36.020
often around technology, around social,
impact of social.

00:06:36.920 --> 00:06:40.880
We're seeing so much of this right now
with what's going on in the world.

00:06:40.880 --> 00:06:45.290
Facebook Live, how that's
absolutely changing reporting.

00:06:45.290 --> 00:06:46.340
Some of the issues

00:06:46.340 --> 00:06:50.030
we're seeing with Black Lives Matter and other things.
So anyways, I spend a bit of time

00:06:50.030 --> 00:06:54.740
there just getting caught up and then
I get on with my day.

00:06:54.740 --> 00:06:57.920
On the other side,
the book end of the day going to sleep

00:06:57.920 --> 00:07:02.540
it's a similar thing. I do a quick check
to see what's going on on social.

00:07:02.540 --> 00:07:08.000
How our customers are doing?
Do they have any problems or issues with our product?

00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:12.440
Interacting with them and then, you know, I call it.

00:07:12.440 --> 00:07:16.370
Michael: Any favorite apps right now other than email and Hootsuite?

00:07:17.030 --> 00:07:22.940
Ryan: Yeah you know I - I... those ones, I'm
definitely big fans of. Uh, you know I - I...

00:07:22.940 --> 00:07:29.720
My job is to investigate new apps so I
have hundreds of apps, uh, you know on my

00:07:29.720 --> 00:07:32.720
phone but I'm playing a lot with Snapchat
recently.

00:07:33.410 --> 00:07:39.590
It's... it's obviously of a force to be
reckoned with and it's got amazing growth

00:07:39.590 --> 00:07:43.580
and I'm... I've been on it for a couple of
years, but the interesting thing I've

00:07:43.580 --> 00:07:44.570
seen is that

00:07:44.570 --> 00:07:51.230
now some of my, you know, friends from you
know 2008 when we started doing

00:07:51.230 --> 00:07:58.670
HootSuite are... that were huge on Twitter
at that point, are also playing around a

00:07:58.670 --> 00:08:02.750
lot with Snapchat and trying to
understand so in that last

00:08:02.750 --> 00:08:06.830
year I've seen a lot more
people join and get into it and so it's...

00:08:06.830 --> 00:08:11.840
It's like people are finally arriving at the
party and uh... Michael: Do you get Snapchat?

00:08:11.840 --> 00:08:16.009
Are you super into it or is it something
you're using because you're curious

00:08:16.009 --> 00:08:20.599
and trying to hear what's going on? Ryan: I
feel like I get it but there was a

00:08:20.599 --> 00:08:22.219
point where I didn't get it

00:08:22.219 --> 00:08:31.639
and you know I... It was... You'd get
into it, and again getting to a party

00:08:31.639 --> 00:08:35.120
too early when nobody's there... It's kind
of like you're looking around and what's

00:08:35.120 --> 00:08:40.219
the point of this whole thing? But once
some people start to get there and you

00:08:40.219 --> 00:08:42.769
start interacting you, start having fun
with it.

00:08:42.769 --> 00:08:48.139
I think the thing with Snapchat is, you know, the ephemeral nature of it is...

00:08:48.139 --> 00:08:55.459
is really important. I think it takes
a lot of the... Like a lot of people,

00:08:55.459 --> 00:09:03.440
their minds get blown on the eternal
nature of things that they put into

00:09:03.440 --> 00:09:08.149
social. You send a tweet, put something on
Facebook, and people are like, "That's going to

00:09:08.149 --> 00:09:11.959
be out there forever." And they get a
little bit worried about that. They get

00:09:11.959 --> 00:09:15.920
worried about people may be downloading
and sharing it and it getting out of hand,

00:09:15.920 --> 00:09:19.370
and so I think that people have some
reluctance. I think a lot of CEOs and

00:09:19.370 --> 00:09:23.510
executives have reluctance around, you
know, doing the wrong thing in social,

00:09:23.510 --> 00:09:31.760
and I think this kind of lowers the bar
of, you know, the risk and ability for

00:09:31.760 --> 00:09:36.470
people to kind of quote unquote "mess up" and
so it lets them play and just - it makes it

00:09:36.470 --> 00:09:41.240
fun. And I think that's what I encourage
people to do with Snapchat

00:09:41.240 --> 00:09:42.949
is to try to play and have fun.

00:09:42.949 --> 00:09:49.850
Michael: Cool... So let's talk about Hootsuite. I know
you started an agency, I believe, and then

00:09:49.850 --> 00:09:54.410
started working on Hootsuite, and then Hootsuite started
becoming interesting and you eventually

00:09:54.410 --> 00:09:56.630
spun it out in '09?

00:09:56.630 --> 00:10:01.760
Is that right? Ryan: Yeah so, that's right... So, you know, I started the agency in 2000.

00:10:01.760 --> 00:10:08.510
Over the years, we did a hybrid
of Service's work to, you know, help

00:10:08.510 --> 00:10:14.870
customers solve problems that they had... And really rode the whole wave of

00:10:14.870 --> 00:10:18.199
internet, from building a website

00:10:18.199 --> 00:10:23.870
you know in '99... Well, 2000-2001, 2, 3... a lot
of people were having that problem, to

00:10:23.870 --> 00:10:24.640
then

00:10:24.640 --> 00:10:32.350
getting it out there, and search, and
that whole wave of SEO, SEM, PPC... to the

00:10:32.350 --> 00:10:37.450
advent of social and, you know, things
like... we built a video contesting platform.

00:10:37.450 --> 00:10:40.990
So we were doing all these services for
for our customers, then we were building

00:10:40.990 --> 00:10:46.690
products, and as I mentioned, kind of
corollary products riding these

00:10:46.690 --> 00:10:49.270
these various waves. Michael: Did it start with Twitter?

00:10:49.270 --> 00:10:55.030
Ryan: Hootsuite itself started with Twitter, yeah. So the "aha!" moment was that we

00:10:55.030 --> 00:10:57.280
were doing services for customers
helping them.

00:10:57.280 --> 00:11:02.050
We had another product which was a
video contesting platform. We would help

00:11:02.050 --> 00:11:06.490
people with all of the logistics around
running a video contest online, and we

00:11:06.490 --> 00:11:11.110
found that our customers wanted us to
help promote the contest on social. And

00:11:11.110 --> 00:11:15.250
so we would use social as a channel and there just
wasn't a good tool at that time to go

00:11:15.250 --> 00:11:20.290
and message into social to help share
content for multiple team members and

00:11:20.290 --> 00:11:24.280
multiple social networks. And so that was
the "aha!" moment... Michael: Is this '08-

00:11:24.280 --> 00:11:28.510
'09? Ryan: Yeah, it's '08. Michael: So this is a year and a half or two

00:11:28.510 --> 00:11:30.970
years after Twitter itself was founded?

00:11:30.970 --> 00:11:35.770
Ryan: Yeah, yeah. Michael: That's amazing, so you guys
were you the first one to really kind of

00:11:35.770 --> 00:11:39.880
create a system of people where can easily
manage channels and talk...

00:11:39.880 --> 00:11:45.280
Ryan: Yeah there were... there were other products
out there, so Tweetdeck, Seesmic, CoTweet

00:11:45.280 --> 00:11:48.250
all were kind of out in the market....

00:11:48.250 --> 00:11:53.200
We came in a little bit later. The
vision on, you know, specifically Tweetdeck

00:11:53.200 --> 00:12:00.460
and Seesmic was to build a browser- a 
social media browser for consumers, and

00:12:00.460 --> 00:12:06.400
that was never the vision that we
had coming out of the gates. The

00:12:06.400 --> 00:12:10.060
product that I wanted to build was a
tool to help teams manage social media

00:12:10.060 --> 00:12:13.930
and I often think about it as like
Basecamp for social media. 37signals'

00:12:13.930 --> 00:12:15.280
product, Basecamp...

00:12:15.280 --> 00:12:20.290
Michael: So you were B2B and the other guys were more of a B2C angle?

00:12:20.290 --> 00:12:26.110
Ryan: Exactly, yeah that was... that was our play so
we would - I'd say - be one of, if not the

00:12:26.110 --> 00:12:30.730
first product out there, to help
businesses manage social media. We help

00:12:30.730 --> 00:12:34.090
power users. You know, a lot of those power users were
in small/medium...

00:12:34.710 --> 00:12:40.020
But also took us into a enterprise
businesses and- and, you know, the rest is

00:12:40.020 --> 00:12:43.530
kind of history. We've been building it for
the last, you know, almost eight years.

00:12:44.100 --> 00:12:49.860
Michael: That's awesome. So let's talk a little
about customer service, because this

00:12:49.860 --> 00:12:54.540
podcast is called "Please Hold," and I look like I'm in a call center right now. Ryan: You do.

00:12:54.540 --> 00:13:01.020
Michael: Twitter, arguably, is the platform for
customer service in the world. At Service,

00:13:01.020 --> 00:13:05.400
we see people create Twitter accounts
just to complain sometimes, and they

00:13:05.400 --> 00:13:09.180
don't use Twitter otherwise, but, you know,
when you have an issue with a business,

00:13:09.180 --> 00:13:13.890
when you have your choices of calling, or
I guess some people still write letters,

00:13:13.890 --> 00:13:20.730
or fax, or Tweeting at a company... the
tweeting option seems to be the most

00:13:20.730 --> 00:13:21.840
popular one.

00:13:21.840 --> 00:13:24.870
First of all, what do you do as a
consumer, when - when you have a customer

00:13:24.870 --> 00:13:26.880
service issues with a business, do you...

00:13:26.880 --> 00:13:31.230
are you smarter than I am and say, "My time is more valuable, I'm not gonna...

00:13:31.230 --> 00:13:35.040
I'm just going to relax and move on," or
are you super active and you got to

00:13:35.040 --> 00:13:36.780
kind of make it right before you can move on?

00:13:36.780 --> 00:13:44.850
Ryan: so I - yeah, I think it depends... It depends
on the value of the product or

00:13:44.850 --> 00:13:48.840
service, how you value that against your
time, and, you know, there's a whole lot of

00:13:48.840 --> 00:13:54.900
factors there (how frustrated you are, how
emotional you are about the good or bad

00:13:54.900 --> 00:13:57.960
services you've received). Michael: Do you have an example? Like is there something recently you've had to deal

00:13:57.960 --> 00:13:58.800
with that- and how did you deal with it?

00:13:58.800 --> 00:14:04.560
Ryan: Yeah, so I had a bit of a funny one
anecdotally that happened.

00:14:04.560 --> 00:14:09.360
So I think about our product as a
whole life cycle of customers that we

00:14:09.360 --> 00:14:13.350
help our businesses that are using our 
products manage, and so I think about the

00:14:13.350 --> 00:14:18.780
awareness through to a sale, and then, on
the other side, trying to help those

00:14:18.780 --> 00:14:24.300
customers become passionate advocates of
your product. And you know, the example was

00:14:24.300 --> 00:14:25.920
Roomba.

00:14:25.920 --> 00:14:29.370
so iRobot, the company that manages Roomba,

00:14:29.370 --> 00:14:35.070
I saw that... you might have seen it... the cat wearing a shark costume on a

00:14:35.070 --> 00:14:39.330
Roomba, and somebody's driving the cat
around. So that was my awareness point. I caught

00:14:39.330 --> 00:14:42.690
that through social and watched it, it was
funny.

00:14:42.690 --> 00:14:45.780
I then asked friends about Roomba on
social,

00:14:46.720 --> 00:14:51.009
you know, asking them who has one, do they
love it... I ended up acquiring a Roomba

00:14:51.009 --> 00:14:55.449
I then shared and kind of like, you
know, "Hey, this is great." I bought one,

00:14:55.449 --> 00:14:57.160
shared it on social...

00:14:57.160 --> 00:15:03.160
And then - and this isn't a terrible
situation - but around Christmas time,

00:15:03.160 --> 00:15:06.939
we had a Christmas tree, there were some
Christmas lights dangling off of it.

00:15:06.939 --> 00:15:11.860
I came home and my Roomba had just
got caught in the lights and chewed up

00:15:11.860 --> 00:15:14.680
- all wound up in the thing. There

00:15:14.680 --> 00:15:19.480
were needles everywhere, presents
were all like skewed around. This thing had

00:15:19.480 --> 00:15:25.089
done a, like... It was hilarious so I took a snap of it, and I shared it

00:15:25.089 --> 00:15:28.269
on social. I wasn't really...

00:15:28.269 --> 00:15:33.100
I just thought it was funny, I wasn't
saying like, you know, I need, you know, I

00:15:33.100 --> 00:15:38.199
need a pound of flesh here or something
like that. But I I tagged Roomba, and I said

00:15:38.199 --> 00:15:40.029
you know, "Hey, this is pretty funny,"

00:15:40.029 --> 00:15:43.509
and what do you know? Like the team
got back to me

00:15:44.019 --> 00:15:50.379
instantly - the irobot... through Twitter-
team got back to me instantly, and

00:15:50.379 --> 00:15:54.189
like, "Aww, Mr. Holmes, looks like you had a
problem with your Roomba.

00:15:54.189 --> 00:16:00.009
We'd love to help make your
Christmas merry and bright, and we'd love

00:16:00.009 --> 00:16:04.420
to send you a new Roomba." I was like, "You're kidding me."  I had like an

00:16:04.420 --> 00:16:09.490
older model, and so we - I went back and forth,
shared my address with them. They sent me

00:16:09.490 --> 00:16:12.550
a brand new Roomba, if you can believe
it. Michael: Oh, that's awesome!

00:16:12.550 --> 00:16:17.050
Ryan: So now I'm like the biggest advocate of
this product, I'm, you know, talking with

00:16:17.050 --> 00:16:20.500
you about it, I go on the road and I
talk about it, so I guess

00:16:20.500 --> 00:16:23.889
the thing that they did paid off.

00:16:23.889 --> 00:16:29.470
But, you know, I think, great example
of a super smart brand that is actively

00:16:29.470 --> 00:16:34.240
listening on social and trying
to help out their customers, and is

00:16:34.240 --> 00:16:36.160
empowered to help out their customers.

00:16:36.160 --> 00:16:40.540
Michael: That's a great story, and that sounds like
it's Twitter's dream to have brands and

00:16:40.540 --> 00:16:43.779
consumers have that experience on their
platform and- Ryan: Sure. Michael: Sounds like a  great

00:16:43.779 --> 00:16:47.800
example. What do you think about what
Facebook is doing, because Facebook now

00:16:47.800 --> 00:16:51.810
is making a huge play to become the
platform where consumers and

00:16:51.810 --> 00:16:55.410
business talk. And that seems to me

00:16:55.410 --> 00:17:00.510
largely aimed at Twitter's jugular, and
in terms of customer service, you know, don't

00:17:00.510 --> 00:17:03.779
go on Twitter this thing that... You're
already on Facebook, you may not be on

00:17:03.779 --> 00:17:06.120
Twitter, just go to Facebook and we can help
you there.

00:17:06.120 --> 00:17:10.199
What do you see in your business? Is that
a thing? Is Hootsuite going to support it?

00:17:10.199 --> 00:17:13.770
Do you think, at the end of the day, one
or the other is going to win or is there

00:17:13.770 --> 00:17:14.760
room for both?

00:17:14.760 --> 00:17:18.300
Ryan: I think there's room for both, and I
think there's gonna be room for more.

00:17:18.870 --> 00:17:21.870
I think that, right now,

00:17:22.410 --> 00:17:25.410
you know, you want your customers, you
wanna have as little friction as

00:17:25.410 --> 00:17:29.910
possible. If you're dealing with
customers that are already in a, you know,

00:17:29.910 --> 00:17:32.640
an emotional state and, you know,
frustrated enough...

00:17:32.640 --> 00:17:35.820
First off, if your customers are reaching
out to you,

00:17:35.820 --> 00:17:39.210
that's because they love you and want to
continue to have business with you.

00:17:39.210 --> 00:17:42.630
If they give up, that's when you should know -
that's a sad point in your business, so

00:17:42.630 --> 00:17:47.610
I always think about this as an
opportunity to help somebody become one

00:17:47.610 --> 00:17:51.210
of your most passionate customers. Michael: I
think that's completely true but I also

00:17:51.210 --> 00:17:54.960
think that brands often make it
difficult to get in touch with them

00:17:54.960 --> 00:17:58.710
because they don't want to give out
compensation when things don't go right.

00:17:58.710 --> 00:18:01.500
Do you think that's true or do you think
the world's kind of shifting a bit? Ryan: I

00:18:01.500 --> 00:18:07.200
think that... I believe that's shifting
and I think that that is an old and

00:18:07.200 --> 00:18:12.870
dying psychology and business model, and
I think that businesses that don't

00:18:12.870 --> 00:18:19.860
understand that the lifetime value of a
customer, the frustration of a 1-800

00:18:19.860 --> 00:18:21.330
call tree,

00:18:21.330 --> 00:18:25.380
those businesses are going to
get their lunch eaten by

00:18:25.380 --> 00:18:31.500
competitors that are more nimble
and agile and deal with the new

00:18:31.500 --> 00:18:34.440
paradigm of where their customers live, which is social.

00:18:34.440 --> 00:18:38.730
Michael: Let's talk about the phone tree, you just
brought it up. Do you think... the

00:18:38.730 --> 00:18:43.260
shift that I see - that I'm seeing, is
people, instead of calling, are chatting

00:18:43.260 --> 00:18:47.970
or going on Twitter. Do you think
that's just a generational thing,

00:18:47.970 --> 00:18:49.410
like in 30 years,

00:18:49.410 --> 00:18:52.669
the idea of calling an 800 number, or
pushing one for this, two for that, is gonna seem

00:18:52.669 --> 00:18:56.749
very archaic, or do you think that people
just prefer different mediums and both

00:18:56.749 --> 00:18:57.859
are going to coexist?

00:18:57.859 --> 00:19:02.539
Ryan: I think that... I believe that the
call tree is dead.

00:19:02.539 --> 00:19:09.049
I think that it will generationally
shift, and it will be, you know, call tree

00:19:09.049 --> 00:19:13.700
replaced going to visit a brick and
mortar, and I think the next evolution is

00:19:13.700 --> 00:19:17.330
ubiquity in terms of where people can
reach out. There

00:19:17.330 --> 00:19:22.759
there may be, you know, I think if you, if you
do the economics on a 1-800 call

00:19:22.759 --> 00:19:31.460
tree, etcetera, versus a truly world-class
social customer support operation,

00:19:31.460 --> 00:19:37.279
I think that A: the ROI is higher on
managing through social, and through, you

00:19:37.279 --> 00:19:42.499
know, various chat channels, whether it's
Facebook, Twitter, WeChat, Telegram, and

00:19:42.499 --> 00:19:43.369
others...

00:19:43.369 --> 00:19:47.239
I think the ROI was higher, and I think
that customer satisfaction is higher

00:19:47.239 --> 00:19:52.220
because you don't have that initial
gating and routing issue. And so I

00:19:52.220 --> 00:19:54.230
absolutely believe that that's the
future.

00:19:54.230 --> 00:19:58.039
Michael: That's because the human answers right
now, on a chat, and that was gonna be my next

00:19:58.039 --> 00:19:59.509
question for you.

00:19:59.509 --> 00:20:03.440
Where do you see bots in this whole thing?
Everyone is very excited about bots right

00:20:03.440 --> 00:20:08.059
now, they got thrown around a lot, and do
you see a world where you're chatting

00:20:08.059 --> 00:20:11.539
with a bot before a humans or instead
of a human? Do you think chats will

00:20:11.539 --> 00:20:14.659
primarily be consumer to human?

00:20:14.659 --> 00:20:19.940
Ryan: Now I think that, look, between work---
we're somewhere in the continuum between

00:20:19.940 --> 00:20:24.169
cavemen and Star Trek, and if we think
about... Michael: That's a big continuum.

00:20:24.169 --> 00:20:28.100
Ryan: There's a big point, a big continuum
there. We're somewhere along that point.

00:20:28.100 --> 00:20:32.480
Do I believe that, in the future, AI and
bots are going to be a big part of this?

00:20:32.480 --> 00:20:36.889
Absolutely, I think that that is
coming quicker than a lot of people

00:20:36.889 --> 00:20:45.230
expect. I think that, if you think
about brands at scale, the amount of

00:20:45.230 --> 00:20:54.049
volume that they have...  They need a way to
help filter and route these messages, and

00:20:54.049 --> 00:20:58.220
I believe that AI is going to
really help us be able to do that. I

00:20:58.220 --> 00:21:01.129
believe that's going to be to the
benefit of everybody. I think that that

00:21:01.129 --> 00:21:04.249
will be to the benefit of of
consumers because they'll be able to get

00:21:04.249 --> 00:21:05.430
instantly

00:21:05.430 --> 00:21:08.730
routed to the right person, and 
intelligently.

00:21:09.450 --> 00:21:12.870
I think that we're, you know, we're
not there

00:21:12.870 --> 00:21:16.110
exactly today, but I think that will come
very quickly.

00:21:16.110 --> 00:21:20.520
Michael: Yeah, I totally agree, and from brands
that I've talked to, they tell me that it

00:21:20.520 --> 00:21:25.410
costs them a fraction of the amount to
handle customer service issues, or any issue, via

00:21:25.410 --> 00:21:29.850
chat than it does via the phone, which is
ironic because most people our age would

00:21:29.850 --> 00:21:31.020
prefer to chat

00:21:31.020 --> 00:21:35.580
than call. Ryan: Yes, and so it's perfect
alignment. It''s ROI alignment, and it's

00:21:35.580 --> 00:21:41.280
customer desire and satisfaction,
so I absolutely agree with you. And, you

00:21:41.280 --> 00:21:46.320
know, we're supporting 15 million
customers on our product. Our

00:21:46.320 --> 00:21:50.460
propensity, and our customers' propensity,
is to solve problems through chat.

00:21:50.460 --> 00:21:55.530
Best for the customer, and also we
can multi-thread support issues through

00:21:55.530 --> 00:21:59.640
chat with our customers, so we can have
an agent that works on two or three chat

00:21:59.640 --> 00:22:04.080
windows. If you're on the phone, you can
only handle one, so again, ROI is much

00:22:04.080 --> 00:22:04.800
higher.

00:22:04.800 --> 00:22:08.910
Michael: That's awesome. In terms of what
HootSuite does,

00:22:08.910 --> 00:22:13.020
we've noticed that you guys are very
well- you're very friendly on Twitter, which is

00:22:13.020 --> 00:22:18.060
not surprising, but your team really
kind of like introduces themselves and

00:22:18.060 --> 00:22:20.670
shares pictures of themselves, and
it really like personalize the experience,

00:22:20.670 --> 00:22:23.880
whereas, think of a bot. A bot is
very impersonal.

00:22:24.570 --> 00:22:28.890
How important do you think that personal
touch is, and is it possible for bots to

00:22:28.890 --> 00:22:31.290
have personalities and be personal?

00:22:31.290 --> 00:22:37.020
Ryan: Yeah, think that the personal touch is
important, and I think bots can

00:22:37.020 --> 00:22:42.240
have personality, and I think that that
will only evolve more and more over time.

00:22:42.240 --> 00:22:48.060
I think that we're going to start to see
directories of bots where people are

00:22:48.060 --> 00:22:51.660
creating different bots where, you know,
they'll be a service bot, they'll be

00:22:51.660 --> 00:22:57.450
an Australian service bot, they'll be,
you know, pick your flavor pick your

00:22:57.450 --> 00:22:58.410
accent,

00:22:58.410 --> 00:23:06.120
pick your, you know, your cultural
norms, and those will all be wired into a

00:23:06.120 --> 00:23:11.790
bot personality so that, you know,
they're responding to you in a less

00:23:11.790 --> 00:23:15.840
homogeneous way, and more specialized to
you and maybe your interests and

00:23:16.370 --> 00:23:21.890
who you are. And that's pretty amazing,
and I think that that actually, at

00:23:21.890 --> 00:23:25.370
some point in the future, will go above
and beyond what a human could do out of

00:23:25.370 --> 00:23:27.680
the gates, and so I'm

00:23:27.680 --> 00:23:32.840
absolutely, you know, bullish on this
as a future for customer support. Michael: To

00:23:32.840 --> 00:23:36.980
me, that sort of reminds me of when you're really
pissed off about a situation, and

00:23:36.980 --> 00:23:40.640
someone just catches you off guard a
little, makes a joke or makes a comment

00:23:40.640 --> 00:23:41.870
or something.

00:23:41.870 --> 00:23:45.380
All of a sudden, they can defuse it a bit,
and it seems like if your bot was

00:23:45.380 --> 00:23:48.050
messing around or being funny,

00:23:48.050 --> 00:23:50.870
you have that as a potential a little bit as
opposed to someone just yelling and

00:23:50.870 --> 00:23:51.530
screaming.

00:23:51.530 --> 00:23:56.090
Ryan:Sure, yeah, why not? Michael: Cool. So, in the
last few minutes,

00:23:56.600 --> 00:23:59.600
talk to me about the Maple Syrup Mafia.

00:24:00.170 --> 00:24:04.460
I think a lot of people haven't heard of
the Maple Syrup Mafia yet, but talk to me

00:24:04.460 --> 00:24:05.059
about it.

00:24:05.059 --> 00:24:08.390
How did it start and what is the tech scene in
general in Vancouver these days?

00:24:08.900 --> 00:24:15.679
Ryan: Yeah, so we're based in Vancouver, BC,
Canada. We've got offices globally: London,

00:24:15.679 --> 00:24:21.020
Bucharest, Singapore, Toronto... We've got
people in San Francisco, New York,

00:24:21.020 --> 00:24:24.290
etcetera, etcetera, Australia, all around
the globe, but our headquarters are in

00:24:24.290 --> 00:24:30.530
Vancouver and ,you know, the
technology scene has been I

00:24:30.530 --> 00:24:32.510
think, globally,

00:24:32.510 --> 00:24:35.990
I think, decentralizing a little bit
out of the Valley.

00:24:35.990 --> 00:24:39.290
I mean, I know that you're in LA and
have folks there.

00:24:39.290 --> 00:24:42.679
There's a lot of great startups, and a
great startup scene that's building

00:24:42.679 --> 00:24:46.760
out of out of LA with Snapchat and
others, and so I think we're seeing a bit

00:24:46.760 --> 00:24:48.500
of a decentralization.

00:24:48.500 --> 00:24:55.250
I've been talking about this for
years in Canada about, you know how, we

00:24:55.250 --> 00:25:00.559
can really rise a tide in our ecosystem.
And there's a group in the Valley

00:25:00.559 --> 00:25:07.790
called the Pay Pal Mafia and they
are ex Pay Pal-ers: Elon Musk, Peter Thiel,

00:25:08.630 --> 00:25:15.290
David Sachs, and a whole bunch of
others that are, you know, amazing

00:25:15.290 --> 00:25:18.679
technologists that have gone on to do,
you know, more and more

00:25:19.590 --> 00:25:24.360
amazing projects and also heavily
invested back into the ecosystem in

00:25:24.360 --> 00:25:30.000
terms of angel investing, etc. And a few
years ago, I coined this concept of the

00:25:30.000 --> 00:25:34.650
Maple Syrup Mafia, which would be
Canada's version of the Pay Pal

00:25:34.650 --> 00:25:40.200
Mafia, that we needed a Maple Syrup Mafia,
and so that kind of stuck. You know, I

00:25:40.200 --> 00:25:44.309
think that we are getting a better
and better

00:25:44.309 --> 00:25:48.539
Maple Syrup Mafia within Canada. There's
alum of HootSuite that are, you know,

00:25:48.539 --> 00:25:52.860
out in the marketplace, building
businesses that are also investing in

00:25:52.860 --> 00:25:56.940
other businesses as well, you know, a
number of different startups in Canada,

00:25:56.940 --> 00:26:01.860
Shopify and others that are, you know,
going public and creating a bigger and

00:26:01.860 --> 00:26:06.120
better ecosystem, and so that's that's
the long and short of the Maple

00:26:06.120 --> 00:26:10.529
Syrup Mafia and, you know, a bit of
synopsis on Canadian technology. Michael: Is

00:26:10.529 --> 00:26:15.630
maple syrup Canada's most important
export to the world? Ryan: That and HootSuite.

00:26:16.140 --> 00:26:22.830
Michael: So my last question is just about
fundraising. You know, you've raised a lot

00:26:22.830 --> 00:26:23.370
of money. You're

00:26:23.370 --> 00:26:26.399
successful at it. Where do you think
you're at, we're at in this cycle?

00:26:27.059 --> 00:26:30.690
Do you see it coming to an end? Do you think
we're healthier now than we were a

00:26:30.690 --> 00:26:31.529
year ago?

00:26:31.529 --> 00:26:37.440
What's your take? Ryan: Yeah, you know it's...
everybody loves that question, and

00:26:37.440 --> 00:26:41.190
rightly so. It's important
for, you know, everybody to kind of get a

00:26:41.190 --> 00:26:45.510
pulse and understanding of the vibrancy
of the technology ecosystem where this

00:26:45.510 --> 00:26:46.440
is going.

00:26:46.440 --> 00:26:49.500
And the funding ecosystem absolutely

00:26:49.500 --> 00:26:54.419
plays with that. I think that, right now,

00:26:54.990 --> 00:27:03.360
businesses are going to become better
and better rewarded for cash

00:27:03.360 --> 00:27:10.679
responsibility versus large growth, and
so, you know,

00:27:10.679 --> 00:27:15.600
growth is great, and we all want growth.
But I think, historically, people were

00:27:15.600 --> 00:27:21.659
burning cash at at an alarming rate in
order to achieve high levels of growth,

00:27:21.659 --> 00:27:26.909
and I think the markets are going to
reward that less than they will

00:27:27.480 --> 00:27:28.740
businesses that are

00:27:28.740 --> 00:27:33.960
a little more fiscally responsible, may
be burning at a lower rate, maybe not

00:27:33.960 --> 00:27:37.620
achieving the same high percentage
growth, but have a path to profitability

00:27:37.620 --> 00:27:42.059
and a story for profitability. And so, you know, I think that will reflect in

00:27:42.059 --> 00:27:47.040
maybe less consumer app creation, you
know, the idea of just creating an app to

00:27:47.040 --> 00:27:51.120
get customers or to get
users without a, you know, a direction. I

00:27:51.120 --> 00:27:53.190
think that may start to come into play.

00:27:53.190 --> 00:27:58.380
So, the other side of it is the cash
supply, and I believe that there is a

00:27:58.380 --> 00:28:03.450
global oversupply of cash that is not
going away. I think there's more cash

00:28:03.450 --> 00:28:04.980
that's sitting on the sidelines.

00:28:04.980 --> 00:28:10.440
So, that being said, I think that, you know,
if you look at what people are getting

00:28:10.440 --> 00:28:14.010
in the bank in terms of interest, they're
not getting any, and so what are they

00:28:14.010 --> 00:28:16.710
going to do? They're going to look for a
different businesses to

00:28:16.710 --> 00:28:17.490
invest in.

00:28:17.490 --> 00:28:22.320
So I think that there is going to be
capital and continue to be capital

00:28:22.320 --> 00:28:23.730
on the sidelines.

00:28:23.730 --> 00:28:26.730
I think we saw a little bit of
correction, but I don't think that the

00:28:26.730 --> 00:28:30.090
markets are in any way going to dry up
anytime soon.

00:28:30.090 --> 00:28:33.630
Michael: Yeah, I also feel like everyone is
becoming an investor. I just met a guy

00:28:33.630 --> 00:28:39.000
who's investing money for Kellogg's and
Westfield and brands that have a lot of

00:28:39.000 --> 00:28:42.750
money but have never historically done
anything in tech. I feel like that's also an

00:28:42.750 --> 00:28:45.360
interesting trend right now, everyone's
trying to get into it.

00:28:45.360 --> 00:28:49.290
Ryan: Yeah, I mean, again, where are you going
to put your money?

00:28:49.290 --> 00:28:53.370
There, that's like, it's a high value
problem, and it's actually

00:28:53.370 --> 00:28:55.590
interesting as a entrepreneur,

00:28:55.590 --> 00:29:00.660
you know, we kind of go out with our hat
and beg for money all over the place,

00:29:00.660 --> 00:29:05.370
but you know as a psychological
practice for people that are fundraising...

00:29:05.370 --> 00:29:10.350
I always talk to, you know, people 
that I can mentor, you know, work with

00:29:10.350 --> 00:29:15.870
and give advice to for fundraising, is to
flip it on on its head a bit and

00:29:15.870 --> 00:29:20.040
just kind of think that you were
actually helping these people invest

00:29:20.040 --> 00:29:23.880
their money somewhere, and you're helping
them grow their money, and that's a

00:29:23.880 --> 00:29:27.660
psychological shift for people as
opposed to like, "They're doing me a

00:29:27.660 --> 00:29:30.090
favor by investing."

00:29:30.090 --> 00:29:34.320
Yes they are, but also, you're doing them
a favor by creating an opportunity for

00:29:34.320 --> 00:29:36.130
them to grow their wealth a lot.

00:29:36.130 --> 00:29:41.020
It does, I think, help when 
you you understand that, that

00:29:41.020 --> 00:29:42.640
there's a problem, a

00:29:42.640 --> 00:29:45.880
high value problem, when you have a lot
of dollars, that you have to put it

00:29:45.880 --> 00:29:46.570
somewhere.

00:29:46.570 --> 00:29:51.340
Michael: Yeah, cool, man well I think we're out of
time, but thank you so much for being our

00:29:51.340 --> 00:29:56.110
first guest. Is there anything else you
want to add? Ryan: No, really proud of you, man.

00:29:56.110 --> 00:30:01.810
Go kill it. Michael: Thank you, have a good
day and we'll talk soon. Ryan: Alright, take care, man.

00:30:01.810 --> 00:30:02.310
 

